#{[#edb/ref 17592186049340 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125390} :activity/id #uuid "6257a384-69ce-502a-8a3f-5b199a93b00a" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125390 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125780 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ invertible matrix $A.$ Remember that if $A$ is invertible, then the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has only the trivial solution $\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nLet's extract all possible information from this fact using the vector spaces related to the matrix, namely, the *column space* and the *null space*.\n\n- The fact that the unique solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ is $\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ tells us that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A)=\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\},\n$$\n\n or alternatively, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(A)) = \\textrm{nullity}(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- Furthermore, if $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=0$ then $A$ has no *non-pivot* columns, or equivalently, all the columns of $A$ are *pivot columns*. So \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Col}(A))=\\textrm{rank}(A)=n.\n$$\n\n- Finally, since $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its basis has $n$ linearly independent vectors, we can conclude that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^n.\n$$\n\nAll the results mentioned above can be summarized in the following theorem, called the **invertible matrix theorem**:\n\n*Let $A$ be an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n![](images/7e/7ef5d72a30dd7f79f6182c4d78d705a7771f9f90ff0bb50f6d539614cb1e5b4d.svg)" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125592}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125391 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124511 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124320 :question/id #uuid "266a9a4e-cb11-5e4a-a2ac-c2e734966372" :question/math-academy-id "e-5424" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix, and \n![](images/6a/6a294de4a5f2ff5755d8ceb5b91bb8920685a1cf2b5e727c5b5fdcfaaf0b89b0.svg)\n\nI. $A$ is not invertible\n\nII. $\\textrm{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^4$\n\nIII. $\\textrm{rank}(A)=4$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following: \n\n![](images/4b/4b42fd4ec8ccf491a1244a648af6fe40ce846fad388685e36d499783a095f8f7.svg)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since \n![](images/32/327a582d15b82ebccda48358062310e6ad5c3bb2689f2024d76a8f8e7e1401a0.svg)\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=1 \\neq 0.\n$$\n\nSo,  according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since the matrix $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{Col}(A) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{rank}(A) \\neq 4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125591}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125591 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124629 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124433 :question/id #uuid "c1120305-c8cb-5f57-97b1-9b31dc059b4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9961" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\det(A) = 0$\n\nII. $\\textrm{rank}(A) = n$\n\nIII. $A$ has $n$ pivot columns" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible ![](images/dc/dc72aea1969143a926fd637d3360bb42116b27545a748923069f4ae732bd700f.svg)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $\\det(A) = 0.$* $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\nSince $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ must be invertible, which means $\\det(A) \\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $\\textrm{rank}(A) = n.$* $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\textrm{rank}(A)=n.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $A$ has $n$ pivot columns.* $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\textrm{dim(Col}(A))=n,$ which means that $A$ has exactly $n$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125592 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124630 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124434 :question/id #uuid "fff1daf8-5a1f-54c9-90d7-87710ca00d23" :question/math-academy-id "e-9962" :question/problem "![](images/3e/3e58a5f09c9060932ee854feeec0733dc9432c2de658308e3ebdccdd0ef89e5f.svg)\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix. What should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n![](images/21/21455a41af5743da6f9f4de5e267c17c6037b929a01ba894cfd91530dbb39986.svg)\n\nThe missing expression is $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A)).$"} :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125391}}] :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124685}] [#edb/ref 17592186051085 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134812} :activity/id #uuid "e5c7e80a-2ee4-5b4a-a66d-c98773047176" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134812 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135103 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3,$ expressed in terms of its component functions as\n\n$$\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = P(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf j + R(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf k.$$\n\nIf the first partial derivatives of $P, Q,$ and $R$ all exist, then the **curl** of $\\mathbf F,$ denoted $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F,$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ given by\n\n$$\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial R}{\\partial y} - \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial z} \\right)\\,\\mathbf i + \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial z} - \\dfrac{\\partial R}{\\partial x}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j + \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k.$$\n\nThe formula above is pretty tough to remember. Luckily, there is an easier way. If we define the differential operator $\\nabla$ (a.k.a. the \"del\" operator) as\n\n$$\\nabla = \\mathbf i\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial x} + \\mathbf j\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial y} + \\mathbf k\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial z}$$\n\nthen the curl of $\\mathbf F$ is given by the cross product\n\n$$\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla \\times\\mathbf F.$$\n\nExpressing the quantity on the right-hand side as a determinant, we have\n\n$$\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla \\times\\mathbf F = \\left| \\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{aligned} \\right|.$$\n\nFor example, suppose that the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=xz\\,\\mathbf{i}+ xy\\,\\mathbf{j}+yz\\,\\mathbf{k}.$$\n\nThen we can calculate $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}$ using the determinant formula as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ xz & xy & yz\\end{aligned}| \\\\ & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xz)]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)]k \\\\ & =(z-0)\\,i-(0-x)\\,j+(y-0)\\,k \\\\ & =z\\,i+x\\,j+y\\,k\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134845}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134823 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133877 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133693 :question/id #uuid "4e4236a9-d99f-50b1-803a-ab3aea5a673e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8743" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x^2\\,\\mathbf{j} - 2z\\,\\mathbf{k}.$ Evaluate $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}$ at the point  $\\left(-1,0,2\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}+R\\,\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the curl of $\\mathbf F$ is the vector field given by\n\n![](images/88/888b12c4f687dba1199a82d7e3a5e6897e7599ea812753423de5a23ffd756888.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ y & {x}^{2} & -2z\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2z)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2})]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2z)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y)]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)]k \\\\  & =(0-0)i-(0-0)j+(2x-1)k \\\\  & =(2x-1)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm {curl}\\, \\mathbf{F}$ at the point $\\left(-1,0,2\\right),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\text{curl}\\,F)(-1,0,2) & =(2(-1)-1)\\,k=-3\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Curl of a Vector Field"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134874}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134827 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133881 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133697 :question/id #uuid "cb99a3e1-9cd1-5335-b20c-01ad598d14bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-8929" :question/problem "![](images/71/71c58bf1b0fe4259ac50633a457f6a76639c0a0c188b66cce5e0fc3c7e5b0329.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\mathbf 0$\n\n- $\\textrm{curl}\\, \\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0$\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational\n\n- $\\textrm{curl}\\, \\mathbf F$ points in the positive $z$-direction." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $\\mathbf F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $\\mathbf F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq  \\mathbf 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F =  \\mathbf 0.$\n\n![](images/86/86aee33cdfa96ac0a61468cc9657e791e399f31c024cdbc3a932193dc897d534.png)\n\nIf $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\mathbf 0$ everywhere in $\\mathbb R^3$, we say that $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, whereas statement II is true. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate counterclockwise. Therefore, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0.$\n\n- Statements III is false. Since $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0,$ the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is not irrotational.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point upward. Therefore, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ points upward, in the direction of increasing $z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134843 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135115 :tutorial/content "We know how to compute the curl of a vector field. Our goal now is to develop an intuitive understanding of what the curl of a vector field measures.\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ shown below. Assume that $\\mathbf F$ is independent of $z.$ \n\n![](images/13/13db0656977bb206ccca66fd25fee08ee2b3c8724173b74aeb4e856109ce0be5.png)\n\nIt's helpful to imagine that this vector field represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. \n\nNow, we pick an arbitrary point in the $xy$-plane and place a paddlewheel at our point with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction (the direction of the curl).\n\n![](images/0e/0eb70168474799d1b94311f444aef72e1904b0c8e13042ba1ba182cd23e57dba.png)\n\nThe curl of the vector field at our point measures how much the paddlewheel rotates due to the action of the water. There are two possible cases, illustrated below.\n\n![](images/86/86aee33cdfa96ac0a61468cc9657e791e399f31c024cdbc3a932193dc897d534.png)\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl. In other words, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq  \\mathbf 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F =  \\mathbf 0.$ In this case, we say that $\\mathbf F$ is **irrotational**.\n\nFor the particular point we've chosen in our example, we can see that the action of the water will cause the paddleboard to rotate counterclockwise.\n\n![](images/96/96bfb9c08d2d5ea0c119e8e4ee6a54e8aaa9fee0eabe8fb13b7c65cc586c3094.png)\n\nSince the water causes the paddleboard to rotate, we must have $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0.$\n\nFinally, note that if $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F =\\mathbf 0,$ then it does not point in any particular direction." :tutorial/title "Physical Interpretation of the Curl"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134846}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134845 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135117 :tutorial/content "We can only compute the curl of a *vector* field, and a vector field's curl is also a vector field. So if $\\mathbf G = \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F,$ then we have the following map:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\,\\,\\,\\mathbf F(x,y,z)\\,\\,\\,}_{\\textrm{vector field}} \\mapsto\\underbrace{\\,\\,\\,\\mathbf G(x,y,z)\\,\\,\\,}_{\\textrm{vector field}}\n$$\n\nIn contrast, if $f(x,y, z)$ is a scalar field, then the statement $\\textrm{curl}\\, f$ has no meaning.\n\nThe curl of a vector field at a point $P$ gives us helpful information about the rotational behavior of the vector field at $P.$ We'll describe this in more detail shortly." :tutorial/title "Some Notes About the Curl of a Vector Field"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134823}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134846 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135118 :tutorial/content "In general, when we compute the curl of a vector field at a point, the result is a vector. This vector gives us two pieces of information about the rotation of a paddleboard placed at that point:\n\n- The magnitude of the curl vector indicates the speed of the rotation of the paddleboard.\n\n- The direction of the curl vector gives us the *axis of rotation* on which the paddlewheel should be placed for maximum rotation.\n\nThe orientation of the curl vector is determined by the so-called **right-hand rule**. To understand this, let's return to our example:\n\n![](images/96/96bfb9c08d2d5ea0c119e8e4ee6a54e8aaa9fee0eabe8fb13b7c65cc586c3094.png)\n\nUsing our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point *away* from the screen. Therefore, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ also points away from the screen, in the direction of increasing $z.$ \n\nThe situation is reversed when the rotation of the vector field is clockwise:\n\n![](images/4b/4b13f2a96f05124184ad8b7f2007b2267a55679b55e5ed6344a2609696abe0be.png)\n\nUsing our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (clockwise), our thumb will point *into* the screen. Therefore, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ also points into from the screen, in the direction of decreasing $z.$" :tutorial/title "The Right-Hand Rule"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134847}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134847 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135119 :tutorial/content "The following two vector fields would both cause a paddleboard to rotate counterclockwise. Therefore, their curl is nonzero.\n\n![](images/92/92654626dae292c06a227622c6eb590df6e7c898eebe1c06d2b3cd57630dfea0.png)\n\nThe following two vector fields would both cause a paddleboard to rotate clockwise. Therefore, their curl is nonzero.\n\n![](images/2c/2cda9e8468dff1fdfe3d60c4069054f1f85ea4c6356075b576f69228def7496c.png)\n\nThe following vector fields are irrotational (i.e., they each have zero curl).\n\n![](images/fd/fd66b65e3b9bdba852e9bfa2826f3950d4b901cab2c76ce0a153491daf338b21.png)" :tutorial/title "Examples of Vector Fields With Curl and Irrotational Vector Fields"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134827}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134869 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133898 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133714 :question/id #uuid "39577555-e9cb-563a-8f5e-8b9ea4b57364" :question/math-academy-id "e-9556" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = e^{x+y} \\, \\mathbf{i} + 3xy \\, \\mathbf{j}.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y}) \\\\  & =3y-{e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(-1,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(-1,1)} & =3(1)-{e}^{-1+1} \\\\  & =3-{e}^{0} \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Curl of a Vector Field"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134843}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134874 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135135 :tutorial/content "Technically, we can only find the curl of a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ If $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2,$ then (strictly speaking) $\\textrm{curl}\\, \\mathbf F$ has no meaning. \n\nHowever, it's sometimes helpful to avoid this issue by considering $\\mathbf F$ as a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ whose $\\mathbf k$ component is zero everywhere. \n\nAssuming that the $\\mathbf k$ component of $\\mathbf F$ is zero everywhere, the curl must point in the $z$-direction perpendicular to your screen. To see why, note that if we start from\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = P(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf j + R(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf k,\n$$\n\nthen set \n\n$$\nR(x,y,z) = 0, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial z}= \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial z} = 0\n$$\n\nand, finally, substitute the above into our curl formula, then we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \n= \n\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nNote that the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial y}\n$$\n\n \nis called the **scalar curl** of $\\mathbf F.$ The scalar curl is associated with two-dimensional vector fields only." :tutorial/title "The Curl of a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134869}}] :activity/title "The Curl of a Vector Field" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134011}] [#edb/ref 17592186049007 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148301} :activity/id #uuid "483b4cdd-3e7f-59a6-bbea-e32b567334fc" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148301 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148751 :tutorial/content "A **Bernoulli differential equation** is an ordinary differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}}+ p ( x ) y = q ( x ) y^n,\n$$\n\nwhere $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are continuous functions, and $n$ is any real number.\n\nThe substitution $z=y^{1-n}$ reduces any Bernoulli equation to a first-order linear differential equation. \n\nFor example, consider the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}}+y = x y^2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x) =1, \\qquad q(x) = x, \\qquad n=2.\n$$\n\nTo reduce our equation to a linear differential equation, we first divide it by $y^2{:}$\n\n$$\ny^{-2} \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} +y^{-1} = x\n$$\n\nNotice that the right-hand side is now a function of $x$ only.\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} = y^{1-2} = y^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = -y^{-2} \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\ny^{-2}\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} +z  = x\n$$\n\nWriting the above in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} -z=-x.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use integrating factors to solve it." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148324}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148321 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147453 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147390 :question/id #uuid "d1946905-5833-58c2-9a0a-4dfd04eb922b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8963" :question/problem "The substitution $z=y^m$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + x^3y= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form $z'(x) + P(x)z(x) = Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = x^3, \\qquad q(x) = 1, \\qquad n=-\\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the differential equation by $y^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\ny^{1/2} \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + x^3y^{3/2}= 1.\\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} =  y^{3/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = \\dfrac{3}{2}y^{1/2} \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ny^{1/2}\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(\\ast)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + x^3\\,z = 1\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{3}{2}x^3z = \\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Bernoulli Differential Equation to First Order Linear"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148322}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148322 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147454 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147391 :question/id #uuid "047ea3c1-8ebf-5e26-9f28-c5a87e7cf997" :question/math-academy-id "e-8965" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{y}{x}  = \\dfrac{x^2}{y} .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad q(x) = x^2, \\qquad n=-1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by $y^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\ny \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + \\dfrac{y^{2}}{x}= x^2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} =  y^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 2y \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ny\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{1}{x}z  = x^2\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{2}{x} z\n\t\t=2x^2\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=2{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}z) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (2{x}^{2}) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2xz & =2{x}^{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{2}) & =2{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{x}^{2} & =\\frac{2{x}^{5}}{5}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{2{x}^{5}+C}{5{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5C_1.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z=y^{2}.$ This results in the general solution\n\n$$\ny^{ 2} =\\dfrac{2x^5 + C}{5x^2}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Bernoulli Differential Equation"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148324 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147456 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147393 :question/id #uuid "b5189ec2-ea1c-5036-9543-b963bd072ab7" :question/math-academy-id "e-9069" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n- $y\\,y' + x^2y^2 = e^x y^4$\n\n- $xy' + x^3y = -x^2 y^3$\n\n- $y' + y\\cos{x} = e^{y+1}$" :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\ny' + x^2y = e^x y^3\n$$\n\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x) = x^2, \\qquad q(x) = e^x, \\qquad n=3.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\ny' + x^2y = -xy^3\n$$\n\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x) = x^2, \\qquad q(x) =-x, \\qquad n=3.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $q(x)y^n$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bernoulli Differential Equations"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148321}}] :activity/title "Bernoulli Differential Equations" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147701}] [#edb/ref 17592186048978 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184937} :activity/id #uuid "45b54b13-346d-538c-876e-285764d59813" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184937 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185320 :tutorial/content "When we reduce a matrix to row echelon form, the number of *pivot* columns plus the number of *non-pivot* columns always equals the total number of columns of the matrix. \n\nBy translating this into the language of vector spaces, we get the **rank-nullity theorem**. The rank-nullity theorem relates the rank of a matrix to the dimension of its null space:\n\n*Let $A$ be a matrix (not necessarily square) with $n$ columns. Then,*\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Col}(A)) + \\textrm{dim(Null}(A)) = \\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n),\n$$\n\n*or, equivalently,*\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that for some matrix $A,$ we have\n\n![](images/30/30f6472f02a4ac47f285fff05e0cdbf56367acdd18231271dc3dccf0040901bd.svg)\n\nWe can use the rank-nullity theorem to calculate $\\textrm{rank}(A).$\n\nIn this case, we must have $n=4,$ since this is the number of components in each of the vectors of the null space. \n\nAlso, we must have $\\textrm{nullity}(A)=2,$ since \n![](images/b5/b5f38590a71a2f8e1f29a5ba98920a10037737c1ebcbd3fd60f08a3e47555141.svg)\n\nTherefore, using the rank-nullity theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185007}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184938 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184171 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184070 :question/id #uuid "10cc01a6-af19-5340-9743-ade4d5e252dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-5425" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 5$ matrix. If $\\mathcal{B}= \\{\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}\\}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$, find $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A)).$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=5,$ and since $\\mathcal{B}$ is formed by $3$ vectors, $\\textrm{rank}(A) = 3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-3 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Matrix Given a Description of the Column or Null Space"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184939}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184939 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184172 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184071 :question/id #uuid "78547385-d46f-5a2a-bfc1-39e040208ca0" :question/math-academy-id "e-5426" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, and  ![](images/a7/a7171d7bdd2b5c79b55ce6bd204e36304640468bd2ad533ac4032cd5a4ddc2ae.svg) \n\nI. $\\textrm{rank}(A)=1$\n\nII. $A$ is invertible\n\nIII. $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $2$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n![](images/ab/ab1cd4be22e0d3344318108914d506f9fc259446654eefff94a36d38afac3f8b.svg)\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that \n![](images/e7/e7660dfd412f13c5d20dd9e77ec439cdbf2916ebfbccc383d13b5f9ed4032cda.svg) \nwhich means that $\\textrm{nullity}(A) =2.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n = 3,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =3 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\textrm{nullity}(A) = 2 \\neq 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ cannot be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{rank}(A) = 1,$ we have that $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $1$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem and the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184219 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184124 :question/id #uuid "9615d8af-b005-5449-be4b-a7487375c98f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9963" :question/problem "Consider a $8 \\times 6$ matrix $A.$ If $\\textrm{rank}(A)=5$ then what is $\\textrm{nullity}(A)?$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then  \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 5+\\text{nullity}(A) & =6 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =6-5 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Matrix Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184938}}] :activity/title "The Rank-Nullity Theorem" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184421}]}
